id: 779
event: message.updated
data: {"type": "message.updated", "sequence": 779, "run_id": "run_caff408c4d634db7", "data": {"id": "dfb6542d-8c91-4289-b7f0-69c1a6d1fd77", "run_id": "run_caff408c4d634db7", "kind": "user", "position": 779, "revision": 779, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "agent_call_id": null, "data": {"text": "Factual review of the completed round (no scientific content; evidence positions cited from files I read directly):\n\n1. Finding 1 robustness number is not computed by the code that claims it. In `output/robustness.py`, the leave-one-out loop (lines 36-50) never resets `best_diff` per iteration, so line 48 (`if diff > best_diff * 0.9`) compares each candidate against the fixed full-data maximum and `break`s at the FIRST crossing. `Bc_stability` therefore records \"first candidate reaching ≥90% of the full-data max slope difference\", not the per-LOO optimal breakpoint. Consequently `evidence/robustness_checks.json` → `finding1_segmentation.loo_stability.range [2.70, 2.75], mean 2.75` does not measure what Result.md Finding 1 states (\"Leave-one-out cross-validation shows the segmentation point is sensitive to individual data points (LOO Bc range: [2.70, 2.75], mean 2.75)\") nor the following counterevidence paragraph (\"LOO instability ... may overfit\"). The report claim does not correspond to the executed analysis; recompute or remove/narrow it.\n\n2. Finding 4's exponential fit is untraceable. Result.md states \"exponential D = 0.133·exp(−1.27·B) (R² = 0.951)\". `output/robustness.py` computes `r2_exp` (lines 160-174) but never saves it: the saved dict (lines 190-208) contains only `Thiers13_quadratic_R2`. No file under `output/evidence/` contains 0.133, −1.27, or 0.951. Persist the exponential result to a machine artifact or drop the claim.\n\n3. Finding 2 counts and characterization do not match its own artifact. `evidence/finding2_correlation_collapse.json` shows B2_vs_B5 max_correlation = 0.99743 (< 0.999); pairs with R² > 0.999 number 7 (B1B2, B1B3, B2B3, B2B4, B3B4, B3B5, B4B5), not \"8 of 10\" as Result.md states. Also \"optimal scale factors form a geometric progression ... each successive buffer condition having roughly half the characteristic timescale\": the scales cited relative to B1 are 0.36, 0.15, 0.10, 0.10 (successive ratios 0.42, 0.67, 1.0) — not a ~0.5 geometric progression. Fix the count and reword or remove the geometric claim so it matches the saved scales.\n\n4. Workplace15 predictor axis is log-transformed but the report treats it as commensurable with Thiers13. `output/analysis.py` line 365-366 fits Workplace15 delays against `buffer_log_Workplace15` while Thiers13 uses `buffer_lin_Thiers13` (linear axis). Result.md Finding 4 compares the two networks' slopes and R² without disclosing that one predictor is a log-axis buffer. Disclose the axis difference or refit on a common definition; otherwise the cross-network comparison in \"Counterevidence and limitations\" is not on a shared scale.\n\n5. The independent replication evidence is not cited in the authoritative report. `output/evidence_independent/results.json` exists and the child really executed its own scripts, but Result.md never references it, so a reader cannot resolve the independent-verification claim from the report. Also `working/analysis-state.md` claims \"numeric values match within floating-point precision (< 0.01% difference)\"; the actual child artifacts differ more: meanfield segmented breakpoint 3.5 (child, finer grid) vs 3.6 (main), B1_vs_5 correlation 0.9489 (child) vs 0.9243 (main). State the real differences and cite the child's file in the report.\n\n6. No clean-directory reproduction was executed. `output/reproduce.md` only lists commands; this round's record contains no run of the declared entry in a fresh target directory and no new-directory artifacts. The frozen delivery criterion requires actually rebuilding the final-version core artifacts in a clean target directory and saving real exit states and new artifact locations. Either execute it now and keep the evidence, or explicitly mark reproduction as not yet covered.\n\n7. Delegation timing and delegation prompt defects (facts for the record): the native Agent launch (main JSONL line 126, 09:16:58) occurred after `working/analysis.py` was written (line 119, 09:16:48) and immediately before its execution (line 133, 09:17:03); the delegated task was replication of all four findings. The frozen Skill requires substantive analysis delegation before the main pipeline starts, with final-stage review/replication not substituting for early sharing. Additionally the delegation prompt's data path contains a wrong segment (`conv-88cfef1f-21cf-447-746678c9cabb`) that the child had to work around, and the prompt omits the contract's required reading paths (context/INDEX.md, SKILL.md, task-relevant reference modules).\n\n8. Minor: Result.md Finding 4 says Workplace15 \"p < 10⁻¹⁵\"; `evidence/finding4_real_networks.json` gives p = 1.95e-15, which is not below 1e-15.\n\nPlease fix the above in this same workspace: correct the mismatched claims against the executed artifacts, persist the missing exponential result or remove the claim, execute the declared reproduction in a clean target directory and save its real outputs, cite the independent evidence in the report, and align `working/analysis-state.md` with the actual differences. Then re-run `python3 validate_output.py output`.\n"}, "created_at": "2026-10-16T09:27:20.826524+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-16T09:27:20.826528+00:00"}}

